      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(net): guard host resolution against unmatched brackets",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen a Host header or IP string starts with `[` but has no matching `]`, both `resolveHostName` and `stripOptionalPort` return a partial/invalid result (`\"[\"`) instead of failing safely.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe bracket-parsing branch expects a closing `]` but silently falls through to downstream parsing when there's none, returning the raw `[` character as a valid hostname.\n\n## User Impact\n\nMalformed IPv6 host strings (e.g. `[::1` without closing bracket) produce a corrupt origin check instead of being rejected.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### After-fix behavior (terminal recording)\n\n```\n=== PR #110645 Evidence: unmatched bracket guard ===\n\n1) resolveHostName before/after demo:\n  BEFORE resolveHostName('[::1') = \"[\"\n  AFTER  resolveHostName('[::1') = \"\"\n  (fix: BEFORE returns \"[\" — invalid host; AFTER returns \"\" — absent)\n\n2) Unit tests — net.test.ts ALL 381 PASSED\n```\n\n### Code change\n\nIn `resolveHostName` and `stripOptionalPort`, after the `[`-prefixed branch finds no closing `]`, return `\"\"` so callers treat the host as absent:\n\n```ts\nif (host.startsWith(\"[\")) {\n  const end = host.indexOf(\"]\");\n  if (end !== -1) return host.slice(1, end);\n  return \"\";  // unmatched bracket\n}\n```\n\n### Test coverage\n\n- `resolveHostName(\"[::1\")` returns `\"\"` (new test case)\n- `resolveClientIp` with `realIp: \"[::1\"` returns `undefined` (was `\"[\"` previously)\n- All 381 net-related tests pass\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] This change is backwards compatible\n- [x] This change has been tested with existing configurations",
